<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagarjunakonda</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagarjunakonda is a historic Buddhist site in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, named after the Buddhist philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjuna">Nagarjuna</a>. In the 3rd and 4th centuries CE the valley held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a>, capital of the Ikshvaku dynasty, whose royal women endowed many stupas, monasteries and shrines that drew monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and beyond. Most of the valley now lies under the Nagarjuna Sagar reservoir, and its rescued monuments stand on an island in the lake.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagarjunakonda.jpg" alt="Nagarjunakonda" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sabyk2001 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddhist_site_3rd_century_AD.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Coins of the later Satavahana kings and an inscription of Gautamiputra Vijaya Satakarni show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> had reached the region before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikshvaku">Ikshvaku</a> kings. The site rose to importance in the first quarter of the 3rd century, when the Ikshvaku king Vashishthiputra Chamtamula made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayapuri">Vijayapuri</a> his capital. The kings built temples to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, Karttikeya and other gods, while their queens and lay women such as Bodhishri and Chandrashri paid for Buddhist monuments; Princess Chamtisiri funded the main stupa for ten years. The last Ikshvaku inscription dates from about 309 CE, and the Pallavas may have taken the territory by the 4th century.</p><p>In the 15th and 16th centuries a hill fortress, probably built by the Reddi rulers, made the place important again. A Gajapati inscription of 1491 records its commander, and in 1515 the Vijayanagara king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishnadevaraya">Krishnadevaraya</a> stormed it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>At its height the valley held more than thirty monasteries, the largest Buddhist centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>, with houses of several schools and of monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, Gandhara, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, the Tamil kingdoms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. The great stupa was 32.3 metres across and 18 metres high. Its reliefs belong to the Amaravati style. A palace area has rare secular reliefs, among them a figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a>, and Roman gold coins of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> and Faustina the Elder point to trade with the Roman world. The inscriptions, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmi-script">Brahmi script</a>, are the earliest substantial Sanskrit inscriptions of South India.</p><p>A schoolteacher, Suraparaju Venkataramaih, reported an ancient pillar there in 1926. Excavations under A. H. Longhurst in 1927 to 1931 and T. N. Ramachandran in 1938 were followed in 1954 to 1960 by a rescue dig led by R. Subrahmanyam before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarjuna-sagar-dam">Nagarjuna Sagar Dam</a> flooded the valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Monuments were moved to the top of the hill, now an island, where a museum was built in 1966, and others to the mainland east of the reservoir. The museum holds sculptures, coins, jewellery and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a>, and many sculptures are in museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. The island is reached by ferry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagarjunakonda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its stupas and monasteries, in style, ground plan, brick size and technique, point to Amaravati and <b>Nagarjunakonda</b> in the Andhra region; by perhaps the 7th century the tall cylindrical stupas Bawbawgyi, Payagyi and Payama stood at Sri Ksetra.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pyu-city-states">Pyu city-states · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…dynasty under Ishvarasena established itself around Nashik; in the Krishna valley the Ikshvaku dynasty ruled from Vijayapuri, at <b>Nagarjunakonda</b>, and continued the lavish patronage of Buddhist monasteries; the Chutu dynasty held the south-west around Banavasi; and later the Pallava dynasty in the south-east and the Vakataka dynasty in…</blockquote><a href="/empire/satavahana-dynasty">Satavahana dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its scenes appear in terracottas, stone sculptures, bronzes and paintings, from the 3rd-century panel at <b>Nagarjunakonda</b> to the 9th-century reliefs of Prambanan in Java and the murals of Wat Phra Kaew in Bangkok.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Excavators found a palace site, a monastery and stupa resembling those of <b>Nagarjunakonda</b> in south India, and many burial urns holding cremated remains, suggesting early Buddhist practice alongside local customs.</blockquote><a href="/state/beikthano">Beikthano</a></li><li><blockquote>Their founder, Vasishthiputra Chamtamula, built the capital Vijayapuri in the valley now called <b>Nagarjunakonda</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/ikshvaku-dynasty">Ikshvaku dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
